########################################
#check after installing pod identity
########################################
i)kubectl get pods \
  -n kube-system \
  -l app.kubernetes.io/instance=eks-pod-identity-agent

You should expect the Pod Identity Agent pods to be running on your nodes.
For example:
NAME                                      READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE
eks-pod-identity-agent-xxxxx              1/1     Running   0          2m
eks-pod-identity-agent-yyyyy              1/1     Running   0          2m

One important point
Installing the Pod Identity Agent is only the first half of using EKS Pod Identity.

Later, when you want an application such as Argo CD or your application workload to access AWS services (S3, ECR, Secrets Manager, SQS, etc.), you'll also need:

For example, the Terraform resource you'll eventually use is:

resource "aws_eks_pod_identity_association" "myapp" {
  cluster_name    = var.cluster_name
  namespace       = var.app_namespace
  service_account = "myapp"
  role_arn        = aws_iam_role.myapp.arn
}

############################################################################
#check after installing aws-loadbalancer-controller 
############################################################################
i)terraform apply

ii)kubectl get deployment \
  -n kube-system \
  aws-load-balancer-controller

You should see something similar to:
NAME                           READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE
aws-load-balancer-controller   2/2     2            2           5m

For a more useful check:

kubectl get pods \
  -n kube-system \
  -l app.kubernetes.io/name=aws-load-balancer-controller

And:
kubectl describe deployment \
  -n kube-system \
  aws-load-balancer-controller
################################################################
#And the four AWS pieces in aws-load-balancer-controller.tf are:
################################################################

IAM Policy — what the controller is allowed to do.
IAM Role — the identity the controller assumes.
Policy Attachment — connects the policy to the role.
EKS Pod Identity Association — connects the IAM role to the Kubernetes ServiceAccount.
That's the complete chain you need for the controller.



################################################################
#value.yaml
################################################################
infrastructure/
└── argocd/
    ├── argocd.tf
    └── values.yaml

is appropriate. Argo CD's server continues to speak HTTPS internally.

I'd structure the values file like this:

infrastructure/argocd/values.yaml
global:
  domain: argocd.example.com

  networkPolicy:
    create: true
    defaultDenyIngress: true

configs:
  params:
    server.insecure: "false"

server:
  replicas: 2

  ingress:
    enabled: true
    controller: aws
    ingressClassName: alb

    hostname: argocd.example.com

    # TLS terminates at the AWS ALB.
    tls: false

    annotations:
      # Use "internal" for private/corporate access.
      # Change to "internet-facing" if public access is required.
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/scheme: internal

      # Route directly to Argo CD pods.
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/target-type: ip

      # ALB listens only on HTTPS.
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/listen-ports: '[{"HTTPS":443}]'

      # ACM certificate is attached to the ALB.
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/certificate-arn: "${acm_certificate_arn}"

      # Redirect HTTP → HTTPS.
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/ssl-redirect: "443"

      # ALB → Argo CD uses HTTPS.
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/backend-protocol: HTTPS

      # ALB health check.
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/healthcheck-protocol: HTTPS
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/healthcheck-path: /healthz
      alb.ingress.kubernetes.io/success-codes: "200"

  resources:
    requests:
      cpu: 250m
      memory: 256Mi

    limits:
      cpu: "1"
      memory: 1Gi


repoServer:
  replicas: 2

  resources:
    requests:
      cpu: 250m
      memory: 256Mi

    limits:
      cpu: "1"
      memory: 1Gi


controller:
  replicas: 1


applicationSet:
  replicas: 2


notifications:
  enabled: true
===============================================================================

Why server.insecure: "false"?
Keep:

configs:
  params:
    server.insecure: "false"

because the ALB is forwarding HTTPS to Argo CD:

Client
  │
  │ HTTPS
  ▼
ALB
  │
  │ HTTPS
  ▼
Argo CD Server

This is different from the common configuration where TLS terminates at the ALB and the ALB forwards HTTP:

Client
  │ HTTPS
  ▼
ALB
  │ HTTP
  ▼
Argo CD

You're choosing HTTPS on both sides, which is a reasonable production configuration.

Your Step 8 checks
After:

terraform apply

use:

kubectl get pods -n argocd

and:

kubectl get ingress -n argocd

You should eventually see something like:

NAME            CLASS   HOSTS                  ADDRESS
argocd-server   alb     argocd.example.com     internal-xxxxx.eu-west-1.elb.amazonaws.com

Then inspect the ALB ingress:

kubectl describe ingress argocd-server -n argocd

This is particularly useful if the ALB doesn't appear or its target group is unhealthy.

Final architecture
So we're now sticking with:

                         AWS
                          │
                    ┌─────┴─────┐
                    │    EKS    │
                    └─────┬─────┘
                          │
           AWS Load Balancer Controller
                          │
                          ▼
                  ┌──────────────┐
                  │  Internal    │
                  │     ALB      │
                  │    HTTPS     │
                  └──────┬───────┘
                         │
                   HTTPS :443
                         │
                         ▼
                 ┌───────────────┐
                 │  Argo CD      │
                 │    Server     │
                 │   replicas=2  │
                 └───────────────┘

                    ACM
                     │
                     │ certificate
                     ▼
                  ALB :443

And your Terraform responsibility is now cleanly separated:

eks.tf
    └── EKS

eks-pod-identity.tf
    └── Pod Identity Agent

aws-load-balancer-controller.tf
    ├── IAM policy
    ├── IAM role
    ├── Pod Identity association
    └── AWS Load Balancer Controller

argocd.tf
    ├── argocd namespace
    └── Argo CD Helm release

infrastructure/argocd/values.yaml
    └── Argo CD + ALB ingress configuration

One thing to be careful about: Argo CD's gRPC/HTTPS behavior through ALB needs to be configured correctly. Before we move to the next step, I'd recommend we handle the ALB annotations for Argo CD's HTTP and gRPC traffic correctly rather than assuming the basic ingress values are sufficient.


################################################################
#9. Get the initial Argo CD admin password
################################################################
Your existing command is perfectly appropriate:

kubectl -n argocd \
  get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret \
  -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d

echo

Terraform does not need to manage this secret.

You can verify that the secret exists with:


kubectl get secret \
  -n argocd \
  argocd-initial-admin-secret

Then retrieve it:

kubectl -n argocd \
  get secret argocd-initial-admin-secret \
  -o jsonpath="{.data.password}" | base64 -d

echo
